# Recorded verification baselines for scripts/verify.sh
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Every agent and every human composed verification ad-hoc, so every one had
# different gaps. The numbers below are the recorded truth of what a green
# tree produces. `scripts/verify.sh` compares against them rather than against
# whatever the runner happens to remember, so "it passed" means the same thing
# to everyone.
#
# HOW EACH NUMBER IS COMPARED
# ---------------------------
# Test counts are FLOORS. Adding tests never fails the gate; losing them does.
# That is the direction that matters: the failure this repo actually suffered
# was a suite silently shrinking (a fail-fast run reporting 124 of 488 suites
# as a complete pass), not a suite growing.
#
# Clippy warnings are a CEILING. New lint debt fails; paying debt down does not.
#
# WHEN THE ACTUAL EXCEEDS A FLOOR, verify.sh still passes but prints a STALE
# notice naming the new number. Updating this file is then a deliberate,
# reviewable one-line commit — never a silent drift.
#
# HOW TO RE-MEASURE
# -----------------
#   scripts/verify.sh --full --show-actuals
# prints every measured number in this file's format. Copy the ones you intend
# to move. Do not lower a floor to make a red tree green: a floor that dropped
# means tests were deleted, which is the finding, not the fix.
#
# COUNTING A LOG BY HAND — USE awk, NOT `grep -o` (added 2026-07-31)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The `root-full` stage FAILS on any box lacking the `/home/ubuntu/...` PCGen
# fixtures (see BASELINE_ROOT_FULL_TESTS below), and verify.sh prints a
# MEASURED block only for stages that PASSED. So that one number has always
# been re-derived by hand from the stage's log, and the notes below record the
# command each revision used:
#
#   grep -oP '^test result: \w+\. \K\d+' root-full.log | paste -sd+ | bc
#
# THAT COMMAND IS NOT SAFE IN EVERY SHELL, and it under-reports silently rather
# than failing. Some agent harnesses shim `grep` to `ugrep`; ugrep 7.5.0's `-o`
# emitted 526 of the 527 matching lines on a real 9279-line root-full.log,
# dropping one `2 passed` and reporting 5823 where the truth is 5825. `grep -c`
# and `grep -n` both matched all 527 on the same file, so nothing looked wrong.
# It reproduced identically on three consecutive runs — this is a quiet wrong
# answer, not a flake.
#
# Count with awk instead. This is byte-for-byte the counter verify.sh itself
# gates on (`sum_passed`, verify.sh:179), so a hand count and the gate can no
# longer disagree:
#
#   awk '/^test result:/ { for (i=1;i<=NF;i++) if ($(i+1)=="passed;") total+=$i } \
#        END { print total+0 }' root-full.log
#   awk '/^     Running /{n++} END{print n+0}' root-full.log
#
# Cross-check with a third implementation before moving a floor. On the
# 2026-07-31 reach-closure sweep, awk, GNU grep 3.11 and a python re-parse all
# returned 5825/526 on BOTH full runs; only the ugrep-shimmed `grep -o`
# dissented. `command grep` bypasses a shell-function shim if you need GNU grep
# specifically.

